<section class="section" id="analysis">
  <p class="eyebrow">On-board AI</p>
  <h2 class="section-title">Machine learning that runs where the astronaut is.</h2>
  <p>
    The multispectral sensor on the astronaut's wearable streams its readings to a server
    on board the spacecraft. There, a machine-learning model learns that astronaut's own
    patterns and reads every new window against them. Nothing has to reach Earth for an
    answer to come back.
  </p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <h3>Can this reading be trusted?</h3>
      <p>
        Lost contact, missing data and motion are detected first. An unreliable window is
        never analyzed; the astronaut is asked to adjust the sensor and measure again.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>How does it compare with this person?</h3>
      <p>
        Readings are compared with the astronaut's own reference for the current activity:
        rest, exercise or recovery. The demo uses a demonstration reference; an operational
        baseline requires longitudinal data.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Does the change persist?</h3>
      <p>
        A single odd reading is not an alert. Spectra asks for verification only when a
        deviation holds across consecutive windows, and it says which signal changed and
        for how long.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
  <div class="status">
    <div class="status-card">
      <p class="status-label">Designed for the product</p>
      <h3>A compact anomaly-detection model</h3>
      <p>
        A small neural network, trained beforehand on each astronaut's reference sessions
        and run on the on-board server for inference only. It takes pulse, motion, skin
        temperature and the multispectral channels together, so it can learn patterns no
        single threshold captures.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="status-card">
      <p class="status-label">Running in this demo</p>
      <h3>A statistical baseline</h3>
      <p>
        A robust deviation score with a persistence rule, computed in your browser on
        synthetic data. No trained model runs on this page. The baseline is the benchmark
        the model has to beat.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
